.TH gvm-manage-certs "1" "2015-09-21" "The OpenVAS Project" "User commands"
.SH NAME
gvm-manage-certs \- manage certificate infrastructure for a GVM installation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gvm-manage-certs
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gvm-manage-certs
manages the certificate infrastructure for a GVM installation.
The certificate infrastructure enables GVM daemons to communicate in a
secure manner and is used for authentication and authorization before
establishing TLS connections between the daemons.
.PP
The GVM certificate infrastructure consists of a certificate authority (CA)
which is trusted by all GVM daemons.
This CA is then used to sign certificates used by the various daemons.
The certificates can be divided into two use cases:
.IP \(bu 3
Server certificates, primarily used for authentication
.IP \(bu 3
Client certificates, primarily used for authorization
.PP
.B gvm-manage-certs
can perform an automatic creation of a default certificate infrastructure for a
standard GVM installation.
It can also verify an existing infrastructure and perform various certificate
related tasks to support the setup of a more complex infrastructure.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Certificate infrastructure management"
.TP
\fB\-a\fR
Automatically set up default infrastructure for GVM
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
Verify existing GVM certificate infrastructure
.TP
\fB\-C\fR
Create a certificate authority (CA)
.TP
\fB\-I\fR
Install a CA certificate and key, use with the GVM_CERT_DIR and
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variables
.TP
\fB\-i\fR
Install a certificate and key, use with the GVM_CERT_DIR and
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variables
.TP
\fB\-n\fR
Install a ca certificate, use with the GVM_CERT_DIR and GVM_CERT_PREFIX
environment variables
.TP
\fB\-R\fR
Create a request for a certificate with the CA basic constraint true
.TP
\fB\-r\fR
Create a certificate request, use with -E or -L and the
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variable
.TP
\fB\-c\fR
Create a certificate request and sign it, use with -E or -L and the
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variable
.TP
\fB\-S\fR
Sign a certificate request, use with the GVM_CERT_DIR and GVM_CERT_PREFIX
environment variables
.TP
\fB\-s\fR
Create a self signed certificate, use with -E or -L and the
GVM_CERT_PREFIX environment variable
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Force overwriting of existing files
.SS "Certificate options"
.TP
\fB\-E\fR
Create a server certificate.
This sets the appropriate key usage constraints for a server certificate.
.TP
\fB\-L\fR
Create a client certificate.
This sets the appropriate key usage constraints for a client certificate.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR
Skip CA generation in automatic mode.
This automatically (re-)generates server and client certificates, but keeps the CA certificate.
.SS "Output control"
.TP
\fB\-d\fR
Print debug output
.TP
\fB\-v\fR
Print verbose messages
.TP
\fB\-q\fR
Be quiet, only print error messages
.SS "Configuration"
\fB\-e\fR \fIfile\fR
Read configuration from
.IR file
(see below for configuration details)
.SS "Other options"
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
Print help
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation was successfully performed.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred, the requested operation could not be performed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
All the below certificate generation options can be set either through
the configuration file or through environment variables.
.SS Static variables
These are variables you are likely to want to set in your profile or
the configuration file.
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_LIFETIME
Days until the certificate will expire
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_HOSTNAME
Name to use for the certificate, if not set,
.B gvm-manage-certs
will try to guess the hostname and default to "localhost" if it cannot
be guessed
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_SIGNALG
Hash algorithm to use for signing
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_KEYSIZE
Size in bits of the generated key, if not set,
.B gvm-manage-certs
will take the default from GVM_CERTIFICATE_SECPARAM (see below)
.TP
GVM_CERTIFICATE_SECPARAM
GnuTLS security level [low|medium|high|ultra]
.SS Dynamic variables
These are variables you are likely to want to set for individual
invocations of
.BR gvm-manage-certs .
.TP
GVM_CERT_DIR
Directory where keys and certificates are stored before installation,
this directory has to exist and be writable; if not set,
.B gvm-manage-certs
will use a temporary directory
.TP
GVM_CERT_PREFIX
Prefix for certificate filename (e.g. "server", "client")
.PP
For a complete list of environment variables, please refer to the
example configuration file included in the documentation.
.SH "INITIAL OPERATIONS"
Get the initial keys and certificates for a node running gvmd by
setting the environment or configuration variables under the
subheading static variables above and calling

.EX
runuser -u gvm -- gvm-manage-certs -a
.EE

adding the
.B -e
option if you set variables that way and adjusting the
.BR -u " argument to " runuser
if your Greenbone user is not named gvm.
.SH "DECOUPLED OPERATIONS"
This section describes getting a certificate from a certificate
authority that you don't control and how the certificate authority
operator issues it. It also applies if you just want to avoid copying
around private keys without good reason.

If you need a certificate from a certificate authority run by
.B gvm-manage-certs
and you've set the environment or configuration variables under the
subheading static variables above use this process to request and
install a certificate and ca certificate adding the
.B -e
option if you set variables that way and adjusting the
.BR -u " argument to " runuser
and adjusting the implied
.B chown
commands the same way if your Greenbone user is not named gvm.

Create a key and request with for instance

.EX
runuser -u gvm -- env GVM_CERT_PREFIX=server gvm-manage-certs -r -E
.EE

adjusting the prefix and choice of
.BR -E " or " -L
to your current requirements.
.B gvm-manage-certs
tells you where it wrote the key, request and certtool template.
Transfer (with integrity guarantees) the request and certtool template
to the certificate authority.

If your request is approved you will get back a certificate and ca
certificate. Place the certificate alongside the key that was
generated by the last command, make it readable to the gvm user and
import the key and certificate with something like

.EX
runuser -u gvm -- \[rs]
    env GVM_CERT_PREFIX=server GVM_CERT_DIR=/tmp/gvmca-charsalad \[rs]
    gvm-manage-certs -i
.EE

passing the same prefix you used with the
.B gvm-manage-certs -r
command and the directory where it wrote the key as dir. If the ca
certificate is not already installed place that as well alongside
the other files, make it readable to the gvm user and import it with

.EX
runuser -u gvm -- \[rs]
    env GVM_CERT_PREFIX=ca GVM_CERT_DIR=/tmp/gvmca-charsalad \[rs]
    gvm-manage-certs -n
.EE

passing the directory where you placed the ca certificate as dir.
After you've imported the key and certificate(s) make sure you shred
the original copy of the key
.B gvm-manage-certs
created along with the certificate request.

If you run a certificate authority with
.B gvm-manage-certs
and want to sign a certificate for someone else ask them for a
certificate request and certtool template, verify their integrity,
review them and if they look like you expect (id est what your own
.B gvm-manage-certs
would produce for the same conditions) then sign the request with

.EX
GVM_CERT_PREFIX=server GVM_CERT_DIR=. gvm-manage-certs -S
.EE

passing what precedes request.pem in the request as prefix and where
you placed the request and template as dir.
.B gvm-manage-certs
tells you where it wrote the certificate, send it back (with integrity
guarantees) to the requester along with the ca certificate
.RI "(typically " "/var/lib/gvm/CA/cacert.pem" ")."
.SH "INTEGRATED OPERATIONS"
This section describes creating a key and certificate from a
certificate authority that you control. You should only use it if you
don't care to avoid copying around private keys without good reason.

If you need a certificate for another host from a certificate
authority run by
.B gvm-manage-certs
and you've set the environment or configuration variables under the
subheading static variables above use this process to create and
install a certificate and ca certificate adding the
.B -e
option if you set variables that way and adjusting the
.BR -u " argument to " runuser
and adjusting the implied
.B chown
commands the same way if your Greenbone user is not named gvm.

Create a key and certificate on the certificate authority node with
for instance

.EX
GVM_CERT_PREFIX=server \[rs]
    GVM_CERTIFICATE_HOSTNAME=remotescanner.example.net \[rs]
    gvm-manage-certs -c -E
.EE

adjusting the prefix, hostname and choice of
.BR -E " or " -L
to your current requirements.
.B gvm-manage-certs
tells you where it wrote the key and certificate. Transfer (with
confidentiality guarantee) the key and (with integrity guarantees) the
certificate and ca certificate
.RI "(typically " "/var/lib/gvm/CA/cacert.pem" ")"
to the other host.

Make the key and certificates readable to the gvm user and import them
on the other host with

.EX
runuser -u gvm -- \[rs]
    env GVM_CERT_PREFIX=server GVM_CERT_DIR=/tmp/gvmca-charsalad \[rs]
    gvm-manage-certs -i
.EE

passing the same prefix you used with the
.B gvm-manage-certs -c
command and the directory where you placed the files as dir. If the ca
certificate is not already installed import it with

.EX
runuser -u gvm -- \[rs]
    env GVM_CERT_PREFIX=ca GVM_CERT_DIR=/tmp/gvmca-charsalad \[rs]
    gvm-manage-certs -n
.EE

passing the directory where you placed the certificates as dir. After
you've imported the key and certificate(s) make sure you shred all the
transient copies of the key you had to create to import it.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR openvas (8),
.BR gvmd (8),
.BR gsad (8)
